The Mathematical Futurology of CBDCs

Why Sovereign Digital Currencies Should Converge on the LIGDI Blockchain

Abstract

Central bank digital currencies (CBDCs) represent one of the most consequential possible transformations of the international monetary system since the transition from metallic money to modern sovereign currencies. Yet despite widespread experimentation, governments across Africa, Europe, Asia, the United States, the United Kingdom, and Latin America remain hesitant to move from research and pilot programs toward universal deployment.

This hesitation is understandable. CBDCs raise questions about privacy, cybersecurity, monetary sovereignty, financial stability, bank disintermediation, technological dependence, operational resilience, and the appropriate relationship between governments and private payment networks. The problem, however, is that many of these debates implicitly treat CBDCs as isolated national technologies rather than components of a future global monetary network.

This paper proposes a different analytical framework: Mathematical Futurology.

Mathematical Futurology treats technological systems as evolving networks whose future value is determined not merely by the capabilities of individual components, but by the interactions among those components. Under this framework, the strategic value of a CBDC grows dramatically when it becomes interoperable with other CBDCs.

The resulting proposition is straightforward:

Every sovereign government should ultimately possess a CBDC, and sovereign CBDCs should be capable of interoperating through a neutral, secure, programmable and internationally governed settlement infrastructure.

This paper proposes the LIGDI blockchain as a conceptual candidate for such an infrastructure.

LIGDI is envisioned not as a replacement for national currencies or central banks, but as a common technological layer through which independently issued sovereign currencies can communicate, settle and exchange value. The objective is therefore not a single world currency. It is a network of sovereign digital currencies.

The mathematical-futurological argument is that the global economic value of such an architecture can grow approximately with the number of interoperable monetary relationships rather than merely with the number of CBDCs themselves. A fragmented world containing (N) independent CBDC systems has potentially (O(N^2)) bilateral interoperability relationships. A common settlement layer can reduce this complexity toward (O(N)) infrastructure integrations.

The result could be a fundamentally different global payments architecture: faster cross-border settlement, reduced reconciliation costs, programmable international commerce, more efficient remittances, improved monetary interoperability and potentially greater monetary sovereignty for smaller economies.

The imperative, therefore, is not simply to digitize money.

It is to connect digital sovereign money.

