I. Introduction: The Coming Monetary Transition

Money is undergoing a technological transformation.

For centuries, sovereign monetary systems were fundamentally physical. Coins and banknotes embodied the state’s monetary authority. The twentieth century transformed money into increasingly electronic forms, with commercial bank deposits, payment cards, electronic transfers and automated clearing systems becoming dominant.

The twenty-first century introduces another possibility:

money that is natively digital, programmable and potentially interoperable across borders.

The CBDC is the institutional expression of this possibility.

A CBDC is fundamentally different from a private cryptocurrency. It is a digital representation of sovereign central-bank money. The Bank for International Settlements reported in 2025 that 91% of 93 surveyed central banks were exploring retail CBDCs, wholesale CBDCs, or both. (Bank for International Settlements)

The question facing governments is therefore no longer whether digital money is coming.

Digital money is already here.

The question is:

What architecture will govern the next generation of sovereign money?

There are two broad possibilities.

The first is fragmentation.

Every country develops its own CBDC platform, technological standards, identity architecture, APIs, settlement mechanism and cross-border protocols.

The second is interoperability.

Countries retain complete monetary sovereignty but use a common infrastructure for exchanging and settling sovereign digital currencies.

This paper argues that the second model is superior.

The proposed infrastructure is called LIGDI.

