II. The Central Paradox of CBDCs

Governments are simultaneously interested in CBDCs and afraid of them.

This apparent contradiction is rational.

A central bank can see enormous potential in digital sovereign money while simultaneously recognizing that poorly designed digital money could destabilize the financial system.

The CBDC therefore presents a paradox:

[
\text{Potential Benefit} \uparrow
\quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad
\text{Potential Systemic Risk} \uparrow
]

The more powerful the technology becomes, the greater the consequences of its design.

This explains why central banks have generally adopted an experimental rather than revolutionary approach.

The BIS found that wholesale CBDC experimentation is generally more advanced than retail CBDC experimentation. (Bank for International Settlements)

The IMF likewise emphasizes that CBDC implementation must be tailored to national circumstances and requires governments to evaluate financial stability, payments competition, legal questions, financial integrity and operational resilience. (International Monetary Fund)

The hesitation is therefore not evidence that CBDCs are technologically irrelevant.

It is evidence that money is too important to redesign casually.

The Mathematical Futurology argument begins precisely here.

