VI. Africa: Inclusion, Fragmentation and Monetary Sovereignty

Africa may ultimately have some of the strongest reasons to pursue interoperable CBDCs.

The continent contains numerous currencies, monetary jurisdictions, payment systems and economic communities.

At the same time, Africa has demonstrated extraordinary innovation in mobile money.

The IMF’s research on Sub-Saharan Africa emphasizes that mobile money and fast-payment systems are already important foundations of the regional payments ecosystem. It also concludes that CBDCs can complement those systems where there are identifiable market failures or strategic public-interest reasons. (International Monetary Fund)

The BIS has similarly identified financial inclusion, payment efficiency and monetary-policy considerations as important motivations for African central banks, while also noting concerns about cybersecurity, cross-border spillovers, operational burdens and informality. (Bank for International Settlements)

The African CBDC problem therefore cannot simply be:

“How do we create digital versions of African currencies?”

It should be:

“How do we create digital African currencies capable of communicating with one another?”

Consider the potential:

[
\text{Naira}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Cedi}
\leftrightarrow
\text{CFA}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Rand}
\leftrightarrow
\text{Shilling}
]

through a standardized settlement layer.

This could eventually transform:

* African trade,
* remittances,
* regional commerce,
* government procurement,
* development finance,
* agricultural payments,
* infrastructure investment,
* and cross-border entrepreneurship.

The mathematical opportunity is especially large because the cost of fragmented financial systems falls disproportionately on smaller economies.

LIGDI could therefore become an infrastructure for African monetary interoperability without requiring monetary unification.

That distinction is crucial.

A Ghanaian cedi would remain a cedi.

A Nigerian naira would remain a naira.

A South African rand would remain a rand.

The currencies would simply become technologically interoperable.

