VII. Europe: Monetary Integration Without Technological Fragmentation

Europe represents perhaps the clearest example of the tension between national sovereignty and monetary integration.

The European Union has already created one of the world’s most sophisticated multinational monetary structures.

The digital euro project illustrates the seriousness with which European institutions are approaching digital public money.

The ECB currently anticipates that, assuming legislation is completed, the Eurosystem could be ready for potential first issuance around 2029. A pilot is planned for the second half of 2027. (European Central Bank)

Europe’s concern is not merely technological.

It is strategic.

The ECB explicitly connects digital euro development with monetary sovereignty, payment-system resilience and the preservation of public money in an increasingly digital economy. (European Central Bank)

A LIGDI architecture could extend this logic beyond the euro area.

Imagine:

[
Digital\ Euro
\rightarrow LIGDI
\rightarrow Digital\ Pound
]

or:

[
Digital\ Euro
\rightarrow LIGDI
\rightarrow Digital\ Yen
]

without requiring either central bank to surrender monetary authority.

Europe could consequently treat interoperability as an extension of monetary sovereignty rather than its destruction.

