XV. The Mathematics of Global Adoption

Suppose each country has probability (p) of joining an interoperable CBDC network.

For (N) countries, the expected number of participants is:

[
E[N_{CBDC}] = Np
]

But the economic value of the network may not grow linearly.

If the number of useful monetary relationships grows approximately with:

[
\frac{N(N-1)}{2}
]

then:

[
V(N) \propto N^2
]

under an idealized network-effect assumption.

This produces a powerful policy implication:

The twentieth country joining the network may create more systemic interoperability value than the twentieth country would create if CBDCs remained isolated.

The network becomes increasingly valuable as participation grows.

This is the classic logic of network economics.

⸻

