We can represent the global economy as a graph:

[
G=(V,E)
]

where:

[
V = {CBDC_1,CBDC_2,\ldots,CBDC_N}
]

and each edge represents an interoperable economic relationship.

In a fragmented architecture:

[
E \approx \frac{N(N-1)}{2}
]

In a hub-and-spoke interoperability architecture:

[
E \approx N
]

The infrastructure therefore changes the topology of global money.

This is perhaps the deepest argument for LIGDI.

It does not merely make payments faster.

It changes the mathematical structure of the international monetary system.

