XVIII. The Case for Universal CBDCs

The strongest version of the argument is therefore not:

Every country needs a CBDC because CBDCs are technologically fashionable.

It is:

Every sovereign government should eventually possess a digital representation of its central-bank money because an increasingly digital economy requires public money to remain technologically interoperable with the infrastructure through which economic activity occurs.

This is particularly important if private stablecoins, tokenized deposits and other digital financial instruments continue to expand.

The BIS reports that central-bank interest in CBDCs has also been influenced by developments in stablecoins and cryptoassets. (Bank for International Settlements)

The state therefore faces a strategic choice.

It can allow the future digital monetary architecture to be dominated primarily by private networks.

Or it can ensure that sovereign money remains a first-class participant.

Mathematical Futurology strongly favors the latter.

