XXV. Conclusion

The global CBDC debate is often presented as a question of technology.

It is actually a question of architecture.

Africa is thinking about inclusion and payment efficiency.

Europe is thinking about monetary sovereignty and public money.

Asia is experimenting with digital financial infrastructure.

The United States is weighing benefits against the maturity of its existing payment system.

The United Kingdom faces questions of monetary sovereignty and international financial connectivity.

Latin America has strong potential use cases involving inclusion, efficiency and cross-border payments.

Their circumstances differ.

Their currencies differ.

Their political institutions differ.

But they share one technological problem:

digital money becomes dramatically more valuable when it can interoperate.

The world therefore faces two possible futures.

Future A: Fragmented Digital Money

Hundreds of CBDCs.

Thousands of integrations.

Bilateral standards.

Duplicated infrastructure.

Persistent friction.

High interoperability costs.

Future B: Sovereign Digital Interoperability

Hundreds of sovereign CBDCs.

One interoperable settlement architecture.

Common protocols.

Programmable settlement.

Lower friction.

Greater monetary connectivity.

The LIGDI proposal belongs to Future B.

Its objective should not be to abolish national currencies.

It should not be to create a world currency.

It should not be to subordinate central banks to a blockchain.

Its objective should be much simpler:

[
\boxed{
\text{Make sovereign digital currencies interoperable.}
}
]

The ultimate vision is therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{One Global Financial Network}
+
\text{Many Sovereign Currencies}
}
]

That is the monetary architecture that Mathematical Futurology suggests the world should begin designing today.

The imperative is not that every government blindly deploy a CBDC immediately.

The imperative is that every government seriously prepare for a world in which sovereign money is digital—and that governments design interoperability into that world from the beginning.

If LIGDI can satisfy the necessary requirements for security, privacy, resilience, governance, scalability, legal compatibility and monetary sovereignty, it could serve as one candidate architecture for that future.

The greatest mistake would be to digitize national currencies while leaving the global monetary system technologically fragmented.

The greater opportunity is to construct a network of sovereign digital currencies capable of functioning as a coherent global economic system.

That is the mathematical-futurological case for LIGDI.
